          "text": "**Observations**\n\n- The first ~67 input rows (image rows 0–1 and row 2 up to column 10) carry only tiny, random‑looking weights (|w| ≤ ~0.04, exactly the scale of untrained initialization). The same is true of the last ~36 rows (image row 27 and row 26 beyond column 19). These pixels were never non‑zero in training, so they never received gradient.\n- The trained (large‑weight) pixels form a compact, roughly oval blob centred horizontally around column 14: row 2 → cols 11–18; row 3 → cols 9–22; row 4 → cols 6–24; rows 5–6 → cols ~5–25; rows 7–12 → cols ~3–26; rows 13–21 → cols ~3–25; rows 22–24 → cols 4–23; row 25 → cols 5–21; row 26 → cols 8–19. Roughly 270 of the 784 pixels are effectively dead. Image borders and all four corners are untouched. A couple of isolated far‑right pixels (e.g. (5,25), (13,26)) carry large weights while their neighbours are dead – typical of rare stray marks that a non‑regularised MLP uses to memorise a few hard examples.\n- Trained weights are small (|w| ≤ ~0.6), smoothly varying in horizontal bands and vertical strips rather than fine texture: hidden units respond to stroke‑like structure. Examples: unit 2 is negative at the top centre (rows 2–4) and strongly positive in a left‑middle horizontal band (rows 11–14, cols 5–12); unit 4 is strongly positive along the entire bottom rows 24–26 and negative in the right‑middle (rows 11–13, cols 21–24); unit 7 is positive on both the left (cols 3–6) and right (cols 22–26) edges of rows 9–12 and negative across the middle horizontal band of rows 12–14 (closed loop vs. mid‑bar); unit 15 is positive in the upper‑left (rows 5–9, cols 4–7) and negative on the right‑middle and lower‑right; unit 3 is positive across the top rows and negative on the right‑middle. These are “presence/absence of a stroke in a region” templates, exactly what one expects for thin, centred line drawings.\n- W2 is dense with mixed signs of similar magnitude (~0.3–1.0) for every class; no class is “owned”